ed the water that had become wine he  didn't know what had just happened but  the servants knew he called out to the  bridegroom most of the time these four  verses and the messages related to them  are secondary to what is the common  subject of that Cana story that wedding  a story you see it's commonplace to  concentrate on the ability of Jesus to  transform water into wine and that's  indeed a miracle in and of itself and  countless sermons have been devoted to  just that topic and I thought about this  message and I wanted to focus focus on  that initially and demonstrate it for  your own seeing you know colored water  in water transforming water and that  kind of thing some people talk about the  chemical reactions that take place and  what is actually going on in that  chemical reaction the power of  transformation and of itself but these  are not the focus of my message today  these are not times in which and things  common happen the times in which we live  or not commonplace at all we live in a  society that accepts war and struggles  to survive  we live in a society that is teetering  on the brink of economic distress and  certainly I see that a 10 percent  unemployment rate across the country and  about 9 percent in Connecticut and  located in places in which I travel with  people who are homeless families who are  struggling each and every day to make  ends meet  we live in a society that reminds us  that some things are as much as we would  like for it to be out of our control  tragedies like the horrific scene in  Haiti my eyes have been affixed on the  tragedy on the devastation that to my  mind is inexplicable  is incomprehensible is beyond anything  that I have ever experienced a hundred  thousand people fear dead you know  because you have seen those stories on  television and in the news and there is  a concert that is to take place to try  to do something to alleviate the pain  the anguish the devastation the despair  and to minister and the way